{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-131266",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of faults in the next sheet of area $40\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}\\,(5\\,\\text{m}\\times 8\\,\\text{m}).$ Since there is on average $1$ fault per  $10\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}$, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, we expect $4$ faults in a sheet of size $40\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$ So, the number of faults in a $5\\,\\text{m}\\times 8\\,\\text{m}$ metal sheet can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =4,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(4).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{4}^{x}{e}^{-4}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next $5\\,\\text{m}\\times 8\\,\\text{m}$ metal sheet will have exactly $1$ fault is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1) & =f(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{1}{e}^{-4}}{1!} \\\\  & ≈0.073\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
